  // Initialise the typing‑animation system as soon as the DOM is ready
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    Typing.init('#signup-form');
  });
</script>
```

That’s it – every time a user clicks into a field they’ll see a short, friendly “typing” hint appear, pause while they type, and (if they linger) the hint will delete itself and start over, keeping the UI feeling alive.

---

## 4️⃣  Tweaking the experience

| What you want to change | Where to edit | Typical values |
|------------------------|---------------|----------------|
| **Typing speed** (words per minute) | `createTypingSVG({ typingSpeed })` | 60 – 120 wpm (fast feels snappy) |
| **Cursor blink interval** | `setCursorBlinkRate({ blinkRate })` | 400 – 600 ms |
| **Pause before re‑typing** (idle loop) | `customizeDeletionAnimation({ pauseBeforeRetyping })` | 500 – 1500 ms |
| **Delay before the first animation starts** | `setTypingAnimation({ delayBeforeStart })` | 0 – 500 ms |
| **Font & colour** | `createTypingSVG({ fontFamily, fontSize, fontColor })` | Match your UI theme (e.g., `#555` for dark mode) |

You can expose these as data‑attributes on the `.typing-placeholder` element if you need per‑field customisation:

```html
<div class="typing-placeholder"
     data-placeholder="Enter your email…"
     data-typing-speed="90"
     data-font-color="#888"></div>
```

Then read them inside `startTyping` and pass them to `createTypingSVG`.

---

## 5️⃣  Accessibility checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| **Label association** | Keep a proper `<label for="…">` – the animation is purely decorative. |
| **Screen‑reader friendly** | The placeholder text is also stored in `aria-label` or `placeholder` attributes so assistive tech can read it. |
| **Reduced‑motion preference** | Detect `prefers-reduced-motion` and skip the animation: <br>`if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) return;` |
| **Contrast** | Ensure `fontColor` meets WCAG AA contrast against the background. |
| **Keyboard focus** | The animation should not trap focus; it only runs on `focus`/`blur` events. |

---

